::PC_U04_L05_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U04_L05_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.5
Question:
Simplify sec(x)cos(x).
{
~A. 0#The product is not 0 because the reciprocal factors cancel to 1.
=B. 1#Correct. sec(x)=1/cos(x), so sec(x)cos(x)=1.
~C. sec^2(x)#This squares secant instead of using the reciprocal relationship.
~D. cos^2(x)#This squares cosine instead of canceling reciprocal factors.
}
::PC_U04_L05_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U04_L05_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.5
Question:
Simplify tan(x)cos(x).
{
~A. cos(x)#This leaves cosine but does not use the tangent ratio.
~B. tan(x)#This repeats the original tangent factor.
~C. sec(x)#This uses a reciprocal identity instead of simplifying the product.
=D. sin(x)#Correct. tan(x)=sin(x)/cos(x), so tan(x)cos(x)=sin(x).
}
::PC_U04_L05_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U04_L05_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.5
Question:
Which identity simplifies 1 - sin^2(x)?
{
=A. cos^2(x)#Correct. Since sin^2(x)+cos^2(x)=1, then 1-sin^2(x)=cos^2(x).
~B. sin^2(x)#This repeats the term being subtracted.
~C. tan^2(x)#This uses tangent without a ratio identity.
~D. sec^2(x)#This is connected to 1+tan^2(x), not 1-sin^2(x).
}
::PC_U04_L05_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U04_L05_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.5
Question:
What is a good first move when simplifying tan(x)/sec(x)?
{
~A. Change both to decimals#Decimals hide exact identity structure.
~B. Cancel the word tan#Trigonometric functions are not words that can be canceled.
=C. Rewrite using sine and cosine#Correct. Rewriting as sine and cosine reveals the common factors.
~D. Assume the answer is 1#The expression must be simplified before deciding the answer.
}
::PC_U04_L05_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U04_L05_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.5
Question:
Which expression is equivalent to csc(x)sin(x)?
{
~A. sin^2(x)#This squares sine instead of using the reciprocal identity.
=B. 1#Correct. csc(x)=1/sin(x), so the product is 1.
~C. csc^2(x)#This squares cosecant instead of canceling.
~D. cos(x)#Cosine is not the reciprocal of cosecant.
}